      "source": [
        "### Reading the decomposition\n",
        "\n",
        "The **trend** component (orange) drifts gently upward across the three\n",
        "years. That agrees with the yearly averages you can compute from the raw\n",
        "series — about 627 units/day in 2023, 679 in 2024, and 702 in 2025 — so\n",
        "there is a slow level climb to account for, but it is smooth rather than a\n",
        "sharp step.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **seasonal** component (green) is a clean weekly sawtooth. Averaged by\n",
        "day of week, demand runs roughly 587–611 on Mon–Thu, rises to about 655 on\n",
        "Friday, and peaks at about 845 (Saturday) and 792 (Sunday) — the weekend\n",
        "lift that repeats every single week. Because STL is fit with `period=7`, the\n",
        "seasonal component is one fixed weekly shape repeated over the whole window.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **residual** (red) is where the interesting failure mode shows up. STL's\n",
        "weekly shape is *one* repeating pattern; it cannot represent a demand bump\n",
        "that only happens a handful of days a year (a holiday-style surge) or a\n",
        "short 2–4 day promo shock, and `retail_demand.csv` builds both of those in.\n",
        "Neither is \"seasonality\" in the period-7 sense, so STL should leave them in\n",
        "the residual. Reading `result.resid.abs().sort_values(ascending=False).head(5)`\n",
        "above, the largest residual days should cluster in a few short windows rather\n",
        "than be spread evenly across 2023–2025 — that clustering is precisely where a\n",
        "real analyst would want to look at what happened on those dates.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a general lesson, not just a quirk of this dataset: STL (or any\n",
        "fixed-period decomposition) separates *regular* seasonality from everything\n",
        "else. Irregular events — holidays that move date every year, promotions,\n",
        "one-off shocks — always end up in the residual. That is not a bug; it is a\n",
        "reason to model those events explicitly (e.g. as exogenous regressors later)\n",
        "rather than expect a seasonal decomposition to explain them away."
      ],
      "id": "zeXF_jqKFGvP"
    },
