      "source": [
        "### Reading the ACF/PACF plots\n",
        "\n",
        "**Raw series (top row).** The ACF should decay slowly and show clear spikes\n",
        "at lags 7, 14, 21, and 28 — the classic signature of both a trend (slow\n",
        "overall decay, because today looks a lot like yesterday) and weekly\n",
        "seasonality (the repeated spikes every 7 days). A slowly-decaying ACF is\n",
        "itself evidence the series is not mean-stationary in the strict sense: its\n",
        "level drifts, so correlation with the past doesn't die off quickly. The raw\n",
        "PACF typically shows one dominant spike at lag 1 and much smaller values\n",
        "after it, which is what you'd expect when much of the raw autocorrelation is\n",
        "actually driven by the trend rather than a rich autoregressive structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Once-differenced (bottom row).** Differencing removes the trend, so the\n",
        "slow ACF decay should be mostly gone — what's left is short-lived\n",
        "autocorrelation plus the lag-7 seasonality spikes, which a plain lag-1\n",
        "difference does not touch. The persistence of the lag-7 spikes after a single\n",
        "difference is the reason a *seasonal* model (here, ETS with a period-7 term,\n",
        "or a seasonal ARIMA) is the right family for this series rather than a plain\n",
        "non-seasonal ARIMA.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What this implies for model choice.** A sharp PACF cutoff after lag `p`\n",
        "with a tailing ACF suggests an AR(`p`) term; a sharp ACF cutoff after lag `q`\n",
        "with a tailing PACF suggests an MA(`q`) term. The persistent lag-7 spikes are\n",
        "the signal for a seasonal term at `s=7` — exactly the period we use for the\n",
        "ETS and the seasonal-naive baseline below, and the reason the LightGBM model\n",
        "gets a `lag_7`/`lag_14`/`lag_28` set instead of just short lags."
      ],
      "id": "emJHVD22FGvQ"
    },
