    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_YXq0_fvFGvQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Section 2 — Classical Forecasting Models\n",
        "\n",
        "A classical, univariate model gives a strong reference point: it is\n",
        "interpretable, needs no feature engineering, and — as we'll see — produces a\n",
        "point forecast and a residual series we can check for leftover structure. We\n",
        "fit one Holt-Winters / ETS model (`statsmodels.tsa.holtwinters.ExponentialSmoothing`)\n",
        "with an additive trend and an additive period-7 seasonal term, then check its\n",
        "residuals with the Ljung-Box test.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Fit the ETS model"
      ],
      "id": "_YXq0_fvFGvQ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "UinVVzH9FGvQ",
        "outputId": "9ed7fe7a-a405-4b76-aebd-d1cf7b986ac5"
      },
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Holt-Winters (additive trend, additive seasonal, period=7):\n",
            "  alpha (level)    = 0.7279\n",
            "  beta  (trend)    = 0.0000\n",
            "  gamma (seasonal) = 0.0000\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "from statsmodels.tsa.holtwinters import ExponentialSmoothing\n",
        "\n",
        "SEASONAL_PERIOD = 7\n",
        "\n",
        "hw_fit = ExponentialSmoothing(\n",
        "    series, trend=\"add\", seasonal=\"add\", seasonal_periods=SEASONAL_PERIOD\n",
        ").fit()\n",
        "\n",
        "alpha = hw_fit.params[\"smoothing_level\"]\n",
        "beta = hw_fit.params[\"smoothing_trend\"]\n",
        "gamma = hw_fit.params[\"smoothing_seasonal\"]\n",
        "print(\"Holt-Winters (additive trend, additive seasonal, period=7):\")\n",
        "print(f\"  alpha (level)    = {alpha:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"  beta  (trend)    = {beta:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"  gamma (seasonal) = {gamma:.4f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "UinVVzH9FGvQ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "H5TNNRi_FGvQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Why this model, and why these parameters\n",
        "\n",
        "This series is a daily demand series with a gentle upward drift and a strong\n",
        "weekly cycle, and the diagnostics point directly at that shape.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **ACF/PACF:** slow ACF decay plus persistent spikes at 7, 14, 21, 28 — the\n",
        "  signature of trend plus weekly seasonality.\n",
        "- **ADF:** rejects a unit root on the raw series, so no differencing is forced.\n",
        "- **Seasonal period `s=7`:** a daily series with a 7-day cycle — the weekend\n",
        "  lift repeats weekly — confirmed by the STL seasonal component and the\n",
        "  day-of-week means (Mon–Thu ~587–611, Sat ~845, Sun ~792).\n",
        "\n",
        "An additive-trend, additive-seasonal ETS (`ExponentialSmoothing` with\n",
        "`trend=\"add\", seasonal=\"add\", seasonal_periods=7`) is therefore the natural\n",
        "fit: it models a smooth level, a constant absolute drift, and a fixed weekly\n",
        "shape with very few parameters. We deliberately fit ETS rather than run a\n",
        "large SARIMA order search — that is the confirmed plan for this capstone —\n",
        "but the same diagnostics that justify `s=7` for ETS also justify a seasonal\n",
        "term for SARIMA, and the backtest in Section 4 compares this ETS model on the\n",
        "same folds as the others.\n",
        "\n",
        "Check the printed `alpha`/`beta`/`gamma`. If the optimiser sets `gamma ≈ 0`\n",
        "(or `beta ≈ 0`), that is a real result, not a bug: it means it did not find a\n",
        "seasonal (or trend) component worth fitting on this particular window, and\n",
        "the model has effectively fallen back to a simpler form. That is worth noting\n",
        "honestly rather than glossing over, and it is exactly why Section 4 runs the\n",
        "same model across several folds instead of trusting one fit."
      ],
      "id": "H5TNNRi_FGvQ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pwfbf3ayFGvQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Residual diagnostics — Ljung-Box\n",
        "\n",
        "A well-fit model's residuals should look like noise: no leftover\n",
        "autocorrelation that the model could have captured but didn't. Ljung-Box\n",
        "tests exactly that. Its null hypothesis is **\"the residuals are independently\n",
        "distributed\"** — so, reading the direction the same way we read ADF: a **low\n",
        "p-value here is the bad outcome** (reject the null → structure remains that\n",
        "the model missed), and a **high p-value is what you want** (residuals look\n",
        "like noise)."
      ],
      "id": "pwfbf3ayFGvQ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 290
        },
        "id": "y6Gunyw2FGvQ",
        "outputId": "e2b4cc8a-c0fc-4b2b-da98-0d9d3106d8cc"
      },
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Ljung-Box test on Holt-Winters residuals:\n",
            "      lb_stat     lb_pvalue\n",
            "7   49.521995  1.792696e-08\n",
            "14  77.143471  9.548365e-11\n",
            "21  81.723812  4.150833e-09\n"
          ]
        },
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 1200x300 with 1 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "from statsmodels.stats.diagnostic import acorr_ljungbox\n",
        "\n",
        "hw_resid = series - hw_fit.fittedvalues\n",
        "\n",
        "lb = acorr_ljungbox(hw_resid, lags=[7, 14, 21], return_df=True)\n",
        "print(\"Ljung-Box test on Holt-Winters residuals:\")\n",
        "print(lb)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 3))\n",
        "hw_resid.plot(ax=ax, linewidth=0.6)\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color=\"black\", linewidth=0.5)\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"Holt-Winters residuals\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "y6Gunyw2FGvQ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Zt1cIYPXFGvQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Reading the Ljung-Box result\n",
        "\n",
        "Check the p-values printed above. If they are above 0.05 at all three lags\n",
        "(7, 14, 21), the ETS model has whitened the residuals well. If one or more\n",
        "are below 0.05, some autocorrelation remains that the model did not capture.\n",
        "\n",
        "On this series a plausible reading is that the first short lags look clean\n",
        "while the longer lags (14, 21) may still show structure — because the\n",
        "drifting holiday-style bumps we saw sitting in the STL residual are not\n",
        "\"weekly seasonality,\" and a fixed period-7 ETS term has no way to represent\n",
        "them. That would be the reason to add exogenous regressors for those events\n",
        "in production rather than to blame the model. Either way, the point of the\n",
        "exercise is that a fitted model's residual *should* be inspected, and its\n",
        "verdict should be *read in the right direction* — which is exactly what this\n",
        "cell does."
      ],
      "id": "Zt1cIYPXFGvQ"
    },
