      "source": [
        "## Section 4 — Backtesting Framework & Time-Based Validation\n",
        "\n",
        "Sections 2 and 3 each judged a model on a single held-out window. That is a\n",
        "noisy estimate: a 60-day window can be an unusually calm or unusually chaotic\n",
        "stretch by chance, and the model that wins on one window is not necessarily\n",
        "the model that wins in general. This section runs every model through\n",
        "`common/backtest.py`'s walk-forward harness across **5 folds**, each trained\n",
        "on everything before it and tested on the 14 days right after.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Expanding vs. rolling — and why we choose expanding.** `expanding_window_splits`\n",
        "keeps every fold's training set starting at the series' first day and only\n",
        "ever lets it grow, so fold 4's training set is strictly larger than fold 0's\n",
        "because by fold 4 more real history has actually happened. That matches how\n",
        "forecasting is actually used — you would never throw away older, still-valid\n",
        "demand history just because newer data arrived. We therefore use expanding\n",
        "rather than rolling (`rolling_window_splits`, a fixed-size window that slides\n",
        "forward): **this series shows no evidence of a regime change** — no\n",
        "structural break, no discontinued product — so there is no reason to believe\n",
        "recent weeks carry a meaningfully different process than older weeks and are\n",
        "worth re-weighting over it. Had the series had a break (like\n",
        "`workforce_demand.csv`'s 2025-04-01 step), a rolling window would be the\n",
        "safer, more honest choice."
      ],
      "id": "pMNLLTQeFGvR"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "PI_CmCgWFGvR",
        "outputId": "7ffa02b1-472f-403c-9330-83cf305d1fba"
