        "### Which scale-free metric — and why not MAPE alone\n",
        "\n",
        "The series has **no zero or near-zero values** (min ≈ 397 units/day), so no\n",
        "metric is forced into a division blow-up. That does not make MAPE a good\n",
        "primary choice, though. MAPE is asymmetric (a 20% over-forecast and a 20%\n",
        "under-forecast are penalised differently), it over-weights errors on small\n",
        "actuals, and it carries no built-in baseline to compare against.\n",
        "\n",
        "**MASE is the appropriate primary scale-free metric here.** It is symmetric,\n",
        "bounded in interpretation, and scales the model's MAE by the in-sample MAE of\n",
        "a seasonal-naive forecast on the **same** training history — so **MASE < 1.0\n",
        "means the model beats simply repeating last week**, directly and on the same\n",
        "yardstick the baseline is measured with. WAPE is reported as the robust\n",
        "complement: a volume-weighted percentage (total absolute error over total\n",
        "actual volume) that never divides by an individual small value, is directly\n",
        "interpretable as \"percent of demand missed,\" and stays stable even if a tiny\n",
        "actual did appear. We therefore report at least one scale-free metric — in\n",
        "fact two (MASE primary, WAPE complement) — and we do **not** use MAPE as the\n",
        "scale-free metric."
      ],
      "id": "jX7DQ2ixFGvR"
    },
