        "### Interpreting coverage against width\n",
        "\n",
        "Two numbers, read together, never apart:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Coverage** is the fraction of the 60 actual values that fell inside the\n",
        "  interval. For a well-calibrated 80% interval we expect it close to 0.80 —\n",
        "  roughly 48 of the 60 points. Above 0.80 means the interval is conservative\n",
        "  (it over-promises safety); below 0.80 means it is miscalibrated (it\n",
        "  under-promises).\n",
        "- **Width** is the mean width of the interval, in units sold. An interval can\n",
        "  hit *any* coverage target by simply being wider, so coverage alone is\n",
        "  meaningless without it. The useful question is not \"did it cover 80%?\" but\n",
        "  \"how tight is the narrowest interval that still covers about 80%?\"\n",
        "\n",
        "Read the printed coverage and width against the nominal 0.80 level. On this\n",
        "series, a purely trend+seasonal statistical forecaster (ThetaForecaster) often\n",
        "has to *hedge harder* to reach its coverage target because it has no\n",
        "exogenous lag information to lean on — so for the same coverage it tends to\n",
        "be wider. That is exactly what this pair of numbers is designed to reveal: a\n",
        "high coverage figure alone would look \"safe,\" but the width is the real cost\n",
        "of that safety, and either number on its own hides it."
      ],
      "id": "-Seo3fS0FGvS"
    },
