      "source": [
        "## Section 7 — Model Comparison & Recommendation\n",
        "\n",
        "Accuracy numbers are only one axis. Choosing what to deploy is a decision\n",
        "across several non-accuracy dimensions. The table below compares the three\n",
        "model families we built — seasonal-naive, Holt-Winters/ETS, and recursive\n",
        "LightGBM — across the axes from `day3/06_model_comparison.qmd`.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Axis | seasonal-naive | Holt-Winters / ETS | Recursive LightGBM |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| **History length needed** | One full seasonal cycle (7 days). | ~2–3 full seasons to estimate level/trend/seasonal; the folds used 730+ days. | Longest warm-up (28-day lag/rolling) plus enough rows to fit trees; 730+ days. |\n",
        "| **Interpretability** | Fully transparent — literally last week repeated. | High: a handful of smoothing parameters (`alpha`/`beta`/`gamma`) with clear meaning. | Low: hundreds of trees over engineered features; importances are a proxy, not a causal read. |\n",
        "| **Interval support** | None natively (would need a wrapper / conformal calibration). | Built-in via the ETS state-space variance (or trivial to wrap). | None natively — needs quantile objectives (pinball) or conformal calibration. |\n",
        "| **Compute budget** | ~zero; no fitting. | Very small; closed-form fit in milliseconds. | Highest: a fresh fit per fold, per feature table, plus the recursive loop. |\n",
        "\n",
        "### Recommendation\n",
        "\n",
        "We would deploy **Holt-Winters / ETS** for this Riyadh / Grocery series.\n",
        "\n",
        "The reason is fit-for-shape plus operational fit, not that it happens to win\n",
        "on accuracy alone. This series is a smooth, gently-climbing level plus a\n",
        "strong, stable weekly cycle — exactly the shape an additive-trend,\n",
        "additive-seasonal ETS is built to represent with a dozen or so parameters. It\n",
        "needs only a modest amount of history (well under the 730 days the folds\n",
        "used), it is fully interpretable (the smoothing weights tell you how much the\n",
        "model trusts the level, the trend, and the weekly shape), it produces a\n",
        "prediction interval essentially for free, and its compute cost is negligible\n",
        "enough to refit daily.\n",
        "\n",
        "The alternatives are not \"wrong\" — they are a different trade-off on *this*\n",
        "series:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Recursive LightGBM** is the most flexible family here and the only one\n",
        "  that could later exploit exogenous drivers (a promo/holiday regressor,\n",
        "  e.g. for the bumps STL could not absorb). But it needs the longest warm-up\n",
        "  history, is the least interpretable (important when a planner must justify\n",
        "  the number), has no native interval, and costs the most per fold. It\n",
        "  becomes the better choice once we add an external regressor.\n",
        "- **seasonal-naive** is the right floor and the right sanity check, but it\n",
        "  cannot capture the slow upward drift, so it leaves the trend on the table.\n",
        "\n",
        "Accuracy is part of the decision but as a check, not the deciding factor: the\n",
        "per-fold results in Section 5 confirm the ETS model is at least competitive on\n",
        "the windows we can measure, and on this same series Holt-Winters is the family\n",
        "that tends to win once it is refit fresh across many folds rather than judged\n",
        "on a single split. The recommendation is robust to small accuracy differences\n",
        "between families because it is anchored to the series' structure — a clean\n",
        "weekly cycle around a smooth drift — and to the operational needs of a\n",
        "planner who has to justify and regularly refit the forecast."
      ],
      "id": "-qTt6VrBFGvS"
